<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swami Vivekananda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1902">1902</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Narendranath Datta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Swami Vivekananda was an Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> monk, a disciple of Ramakrishna, who did much to introduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> and yoga to the West. Before his celebrated address to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worlds-parliament-of-religions">World's Parliament of Religions</a> in Chicago in 1893 he travelled across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as a wandering monk, hosted by rulers such as those of Limbdi and Khetri. In 1897 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakrishna-mission">Ramakrishna Mission</a>, and his teaching shaped modern Hindu reform and Indian nationalism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda.jpg" alt="Swami Vivekananda" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Dziewa at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swami_Vivekananda-1893-09-signed.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chithira Thirunal's Temple Entry Proclamation of 12 November 1936 opened every Hindu temple in Travancore to the backward communities and won praise from Mahatma Gandhi; <b>Swami Vivekananda</b> had earlier called the state &quot;The Lunatic Asylum in India&quot; for its caste discrimination.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Ram Mohan Roy founded the Brahmo Samaj in 1828 to promote a rational, monotheistic Hinduism; Dayananda Saraswati founded the Arya Samaj in 1875 to return to the Vedas; and <b>Swami Vivekananda</b>’s address to the World’s Parliament of Religions in Chicago in 1893 introduced Hindu philosophy to Western audiences.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In modern times <b>Swami Vivekananda</b>, Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose, Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Mahatma Gandhi and many others used it to help inspire the Indian independence movement.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1898 <b>Swami Vivekananda</b>, arguing that one became a Brahmin by learning rather than by birth, gave the mantra to non-Brahmins in the Ramakrishna Mission, and Dayananda Saraswati, founder of the Arya Samaj, gave it a rendering of his own.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gayatri-mantra">Gayatri Mantra</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Thakore Sahebs were noted for promoting education, and Thakore Jaswantsinhji hosted <b>Swami Vivekananda</b> during his travels in 1891.</blockquote><a href="/state/limbdi-state">Limbdi State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Belur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.64,88.34&amp;year=1888" aria-label="Belur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6913 1328 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Swami Vivekananda"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7363 1598h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7363 1598h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=22.64,88.34&amp;year=1888">What was here in 1888? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am proud to belong to a religion which has taught the world both tolerance and universal acceptance. We believe not only in universal toleration, but we accept all religions as true.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to the Parliament of Religions, Chicago, 1893 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Complete_Works_of_Swami_Vivekananda/Volume_1/Addresses_at_The_Parliament_of_Religions/Response_to_Welcome" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Sectarianism, bigotry, and its horrible descendant, fanaticism, have long possessed this beautiful earth.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to the Parliament of Religions, Chicago, 1893 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Complete_Works_of_Swami_Vivekananda/Volume_1/Addresses_at_The_Parliament_of_Religions/Response_to_Welcome" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My noble Prince, this life is short, the vanities of the world are transient, but they alone live who live for others, the rest are more dead than alive.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Maharaja of Mysore, 1894 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Complete_Works_of_Swami_Vivekananda/Volume_4/Writings:_Prose/Our_Duty_to_the_Masses" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The Christian is not to become a Hindu or a Buddhist, nor a Hindu or a Buddhist to become a Christian. But each must assimilate the spirit of the others and yet preserve his individuality and grow according to his own law of growth.&#8221;</p><footer>Address at the final session of the Parliament of Religions, Chicago, 1893 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Complete_Works_of_Swami_Vivekananda/Volume_1/Addresses_at_The_Parliament_of_Religions/Address_at_the_Final_Session" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Come up, O lions, and shake off the delusion that you are sheep; you are souls immortal, spirits free, blest and eternal…&#8221;</p><footer>Paper on Hinduism, Parliament of Religions, Chicago, 1893 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Complete_Works_of_Swami_Vivekananda/Volume_1/Addresses_at_The_Parliament_of_Religions/Paper_on_Hinduism" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ye are the Children of God, the sharers of immortal bliss, holy and perfect beings. Ye divinities on earth — sinners! It is a sin to call a man so; it is a standing libel on human nature.&#8221;</p><footer>Paper on Hinduism, Parliament of Religions, Chicago, 1893 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Complete_Works_of_Swami_Vivekananda/Volume_1/Addresses_at_The_Parliament_of_Religions/Paper_on_Hinduism" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
